---
title: "Why moving_average treats the start of a clip differently"
author: Zwk-tech
date: 2026-11-27
abstract: A tour of stdlib/signals.moving_average — why a transient at the start of a clip reads differently from one in the middle, explained through the running denominator, with a runnable example pinned to [0, 0, 2, 2, 2, 0] and [6, 3, 2, 0].
---

## One function, two different-looking impulses

`stdlib/signals.ltl` provides `moving_average(s, window)`, a running mean over a
sliding window. Feed it the same spike — a single sample of amplitude 6 — and
where that spike sits in the clip changes what you see at the output:

// Impulse in the middle of a six-sample clip:
moving_average([0, 0, 6, 0, 0, 0], 3)  =>  [0, 0, 2, 2, 2, 0]

// The same impulse, but now at sample 0 of a four-sample clip:
moving_average([6, 0, 0, 0], 3)        =>  [6, 3, 2, 0]

The middle impulse flattens into a plateau of three 2s. The leading impulse
starts tall at 6 and decays 6, 3, 2 before vanishing. Same spike, same window
size — so what gives?

## The denominator is the whole story

Here is the heart of the implementation:

    cumsum = 0.0
    for i in range(n):
        cumsum += samples[i]
        if i >= w:
            cumsum -= samples[i - w]
        count = min(i + 1, w)
        out.append(cumsum / count)

There are two moving parts. The numerator, `cumsum`, is a running total that
drops samples older than the window (`samples[i - w]`). That part behaves the
same everywhere in the clip. The part that does not behave the same everywhere
is the denominator:

    count = min(i + 1, w)

At sample 0 there is only one sample in the window, so `count` is 1. At sample 1
it is 2, at sample 2 it is 3 — and from then on it is pinned at the window size
`w`. Before the window has filled, `moving_average` is really dividing by *how
many samples exist so far*, not by `w`.

## Walk the middle impulse

For `[0, 0, 6, 0, 0, 0]` with `w = 3`, the denominator ramp is `1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 3`:

    i=0  count=1  cumsum=0        out=0      (nothing yet)
    i=1  count=2  cumsum=0        out=0      (nothing yet)
    i=2  count=3  cumsum=0+6=6    out=2      <-- spike arrives, count already 3
    i=3  count=3  cumsum=6+0=6    out=2
    i=4  count=3  cumsum=6+0=6    out=2
    i=5  count=3  cumsum=6-6=0    out=0      <-- spike leaves the window

When the 6 arrives at sample 2, the denominator has already reached 3. The spike
is split evenly across three samples, giving the flat `2, 2, 2` plateau. It is
then subtracted cleanly at i=5 when `samples[i - w] = samples[2]` falls out.

## Walk the leading impulse

For `[6, 0, 0, 0]` with `w = 3`, the same denominator ramp tells a different
story because the spike is present *while the denominator is still growing*:

    i=0  count=1  cumsum=6        out=6/1 = 6
    i=1  count=2  cumsum=6+0=6    out=6/2 = 3
    i=2  count=3  cumsum=6+0=6    out=6/3 = 2
    i=3  count=3  cumsum=6-6=0    out=0      <-- spike leaves the window

The running total stays at 6 for those first three samples, but the divisor
climbs 1 -> 2 -> 3 underneath it. That is the entire reason the output decays
`6, 3, 2` instead of sitting at a plateau. This is a startup transient of the
estimator, not a property of the signal.

## Why this matters for audio

On a real clip, the first few milliseconds of any moving-average filter are
computed over a partially-filled window. If you treat the very first samples as
a steady-state average, you will misread a leading click or impulse — the
`min(i + 1, w)` denominator deliberately shrinks the support so the average is
well-defined from sample 0, at the cost of a short ramp. Once `i >= w`, the
filter is in steady state and behaves identically no matter where the impulse
falls.

## Run it yourself

The companion example `examples/signals_moving_average.ltl` is self-contained:
it reimplements the small slice of `stdlib/signals.ltl` used here in plain
Lateralus, so it runs on the Python backend without the v3.2.0 stdlib-import
inlining limitation.

    python bin/ltlc check examples/signals_moving_average.ltl
    python bin/ltlc run   examples/signals_moving_average.ltl

Running it prints:

    [0.0, 0.0, 2.0, 2.0, 2.0, 0.0]
    [6.0, 3.0, 2.0, 0.0]
    [1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 3]

The three `@test` vectors — `test_middle_transient`, `test_leading_transient`,
and `test_denominator_ramp` — all pass, pinning the two waveforms and the
denominator ramp described above.

## Takeaways

- `moving_average` divides by `min(i + 1, w)`, not always by `w`.
- Before the window fills, that growing denominator turns a leading spike into
  a decaying `6, 3, 2` ramp.
- After the window fills, the same spike becomes a flat `2, 2, 2` plateau.
- The difference is a startup transient of the estimator, not a change in the
  signal — and the pinned vectors `["0,0,2,2,2,0"]` and `["6,3,2,0"]` make it
  reproducible.

